-- title:
--   Twisted 𝔽_{q²}-forms of the Drinfeld curve function field
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a natural number assumed prime, and let $k$ be a field that is algebraically closed and carries an algebra structure over the field $\mathrm{GaloisField}\ q\ 2$ with $q^2$ elements. Assume that the coordinate ring $\mathrm{CoordRing}\ q\ k$, namely the quotient of the polynomial ring in two variables $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}\ (\mathrm{Fin}\ 2)\ k$ by the principal ideal generated by $\mathrm{drinfeldPoly}\ q\ k - 1$, is a domain, and that its fraction field $F := \mathrm{drinfeldFunctionField}\ q\ k$ is a curve over $k$ in the sense of [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15): every nonzero element of $F$ is the divisor of a degree-zero principal divisor, each place of $F/k$ has residue field finite over $k$, and $\Omega_{F/k}$ is free of rank one over $F$. Let $\varphi : F \to F$ be a $k$-algebra endomorphism whose underlying ring map is integral (every element of $F$ is integral over the image of $\varphi$) and which sends the images of the two coordinates $x$ and $y$ in $F$ to their $q^2$-th powers. Let $h$ lie in $\mathrm{hSubgroup}\ q$, the kernel of the character $\mathrm{hChar}\ q$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q) \times (\mathbb{F}_{q^2})^\times$, i.e. the pairs $(g,\zeta)$ with $\det(g)\,\zeta^{q+1} = 1$. Then there exist a field $F_1$, equipped with an $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure, an algebra structure making $F$ an $F_1$-algebra, and a proof that $F_1$ is a curve over $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ in the above sense, such that: $F_1$ is generated as an intermediate field over $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ by some finite subset; the intermediate field of $F/k$ generated by the image of $F_1$ in $F$ is all of $F$; and for every $z \in F_1$, applying to the image of $z$ first the automorphism $\mathrm{hFunctionFieldAction}\ q\ k\ h$ of $F$ (the extension to the fraction field of the action of $h$ on the coordinate ring) and then $\varphi$ gives the image of $z^{\,\mathrm{Nat.card}\,\mathbb{F}_{q^2}} = z^{q^2}$.
--
--   This exhibits, for each element $h$ of the group $H$ acting on the Drinfeld curve of Deligne–Lusztig, an $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-form $F_1$ of the function field $F$ for which the composite of $h$ with the $q^2$-power endomorphism $\varphi$ is the relative $q^2$-Frobenius; at $h=1$ one may take the form generated by the coordinates over $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$, and the general case is the twist of that form by $h$. It packages exactly the hypotheses needed by the Lefschetz-type trace statements on the Drinfeld curve, and is used by [`DrinfeldCurve.cast_mul_trace_eq_natCard_restrictAlong_eq_smul_sub`](thm.html#DrinfeldCurve.cast_mul_trace_eq_natCard_restrictAlong_eq_smul_sub) and [`DrinfeldCurve.trace_torsion_eq_sq_add_one_sub_natCard_restrictAlong_eq_smul`](thm.html#DrinfeldCurve.trace_torsion_eq_sq_add_one_sub_natCard_restrictAlong_eq_smul).
